 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public service law, in relation to requiring water works\ncorporations and municipal water systems to annually calculate and submit to\nthe public service commission their water cost index",
    "basePrintNo": "S8225",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8225-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §89-k, Pub Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Public Service Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S8225\n\nSPONSOR: RANZENHOFER\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public service law, in relation to requiring water\nworks corporations and municipal water systems to annually calculate and\nsubmit to the public service commission their water cost index\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis bill would require public and private water supply systems, serving\nover one thousand customers, to annually submit to the Public Service\nCommission their water cost index, a uniform measurement of the average\ncost of water usage.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of the bill amends the Public Service Law by adding a new\nsection 89-k as it relates to requiring public and private water supply\nsystems, serving over one thousand customers, to annually submit to the\nPublic Service Commission their water cost index. The Public Service\nCommission shall develop the water cost index calculation based on\nuniform consumption factors.  Water works corporations and municipal\nwater systems, using the calculations provided by the Public Service\nCommission, shall submit their water cost index within sixty days of the\nclose of their fiscal year. The Commission shall annually publish a\nreport with all the submitted data and the analysis on their website by\nDecember 31 of each year.\n\nSection two of the bill relates to the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThere is little information available to consumers on how each water\nsupply providers costs vary throughout the state. Each water system\nbills consumers in different measurements and utilizes various cost\nstructures which make it difficult to compare the impact to consumers\nacross systems.\n\nThis bill would shed light on the annual costs to consumers and provide\nvaluable information to ratepayers. It would require the PSC to develop\na uniform standard on measuring the annual cost of water in each public\nand private water supply system. This will allow consumers to analyze\ntheir water costs and compare to systems across the state. Water is not\na commodity that is traded or which has prices that are uniform across\nmarkets; leading to varying costs throughout the state.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nMinimal cost to the Commission in collecting the data from water\nsystems.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
